Metal Organic Frameworks (MOFs) are a class of porous inorganic-organic hybrid materials. The unique properties of MOFs, such as permanent porosity, high chemical, and thermal stability, and extreme tunability, have propelled MOFs into various applications including energy storage, drug delivery, and catalysis $\displaystyle{\left[1-5\right]}$ Compared with traditional porous solids, such as zeolites, activated carbon, and mesoporous silica, MOFs allow for the precise design of framework porosity as well as tailoring of the pore environments at the molecular or atomic level.  Unfortunately, the pore sizes of most reported MOFs to date are limited to a specific micropore range (pore diameter smaller than $2\ n m^{\cdot}$ ), which are suitable only for the adsorption, separation, and catalysis of small guest molecules, such as gases, organic molecules, and coordination complexes. The limited pore sizes may decrease mass transportation rates, restrict the diffusion of substrates to catalytic active sites, and can even hinder the development of composite materials with larger components such as nanoparticles, enzymes, and proteins.[6–8]
In order to combat the issue of molecular diffusion in MOFs, the synthesis and development of Hierarchically Porous MOFs (HP-MOFs) have gained significant momentum in recent years. HP-MOFs are defined as MOFs that contain engineered mesopores/macropores in addition to the intrinsic micropores associated with that specific framework. Compared to mesoporous MOFs, HP-MOFs can dramatically improve mass transportation without losing their intrinsic molecular adsorption and separation capabilities.[8] However, the direct synthesis of HP-MOFs is challenging and time-consuming.  The preparation of HP-MOF usually involves one of the two methods: modulation or templated synthesis.[8–13] Modulation refers to the use of molecules that coordinate to the metal clusters in a similar geometry as the ligand but have lower binding affinities than that of the desired ligand, in order to regulate the coordination equilibrium of a growing MOF crystal. It has been well documented that the use of modulating agents, not only can lead to the synthesis of defect-free MOFs, but they can also be used to systematically control defect generation, leading to HP-MOF formation. For example, the pKa of the modulator, coordination modes of the modulator, and even the size/length of the modulator can affect HP-MOF generation.[12–15]
Alternatively, it has been found that during the templated synthesis of HP-MOFs, the templating agents tend to form aggregates. These aggregates are traditionally composed of surfactants or other amphiphilic molecules, which form micelles that proceed with the generation of HP-MOFs. Evidence suggests that the mesopore diameter and location is strongly dependent on the hydrophobic volume of these self-assembled aggregates.[9–11] However, it has been long reported that micelles cannot form unless the concentration of the amphiphilic molecules is above the critical micelle concentration (cmc), and this concentration depends on several variables, including chemical identity, molecular size, solution pH, and even the reaction temperature. For example, micelles formation is not favorable if the molecule has less than 7 atoms and is more favorable with molecules containing 7-16 atoms.[16,17] Furthermore, due to its solvation strength, the cmc of a surfactant in DMF has been reported to be 10-30 times higher than that of aqueous systems.[18,19] For this reason, there have been no reports of templating MOFs using DMF as the solvent (previously literature are aqueousbased).[18,20–27]
Therefore, in order to study the effects of modulating versus templating during the generation of HP-MOFs, we have developed a system that utilizes aliphatic mono-carboxylic acids with different lengths that can act as either modulators or templating agents during the generation of HP-MOFs (Figure 1). By varying the concentrations and the lengths of mono-carboxylic acid molecules utilized during the solvothermal synthesis, it is proposed that the mechanism of mesopore formation (modulating or templating) can be effectively tuned, and different variants of hierarchically porous PCN-250 can be obtained due to the different mechanisms. For the modulation-based mechanism, the mono-carboxylic acid molecules act as typical modulators generating mesopores through increased steric effects. In the templating-based mechanism, the hydrophobic alkyl chains of the mono-carboxylic acid molecules self-assemble into micellular type structures, which act as templates that subsequently generate mesopores within the obtained MOF.
PCN-250, also known as MIL-127, is a well-studied MOF that is constructed from $\mathsf{F e}_{3^{-}}\upmu^{3}\ –\mathsf{o}\times0$ clusters and tetratopic azobenzene-based linkers $(A B{\mathsf{T C}}=3,3^{\prime},5,5^{\prime}$ -azo-benzene-tetracarboxylate). PCN-250 has been shown to be stable in aqueous solutions from $p H=1$ to $p H=12$ .[28–31] Unfortunately, due to its small pore size, potential applications that take advantage of the excellent water stability of PCN-250 have not yet been investigated. Therefore, we have selected PCN-250 as a prime candidate for studying how modulation versus templating phenomena affect the mesopore size, shape, and location, and how this affects the subsequent application of the obtained HPMOF material.
Four different lengths of mono-carboxylic acid molecules were selected to be used in this study: propanoic acid (C3), hexanoic acid (C6), nonanoic acid (C9), and myristic acid (C14). In the first step of the synthesis, mono-carboxylic acids were added to the mixture of MOF precursors during the solvothermal process in order to obtain the As-Synthesized MOF (AsSyn). In the second step, an acetic acid-based, post-synthetic chemical etching technique was performed to maximize overall porosity of the AsSyn samples as shown in Figures S1-S4. In this process, the acetic acid serves three purposes: 1) It acts as a replacement ligand for any monocoordinated long chain molecules/templates; 2) It helps promote the removal of any unreacted starting materials from within the pores; 3) It acts as an etching agent to remove areas of low crystallinity on the surface of crystals as well as remove partially connected clusters from within the structure. Based on this method, the mesopore volume was optimized for the time of etching as shown in Figures S5-S6.  Inductively Coupled Plasma-Mass Spectroscopy (ICP-MS) of the etching solutions (Table S1), Powder X-ray Diffraction (PXRD) and thermal stability studies (TGA) for each sample are reported in Figures S7-S22. Furthermore, TGA-MS studies were performed as shown in Figure S26-S29 in order to show complete removal of the fatty acids from within the MOF structure.
Interestingly, different fatty acids yield totally different MOFs. When propanoic acid (C3) was applied, only microporous samples were obtained, which all adopt the same structure and porosity as the pristine PCN-250 (Table S2 and Figure S1).[29,30] When myristic acid (C14) was used, again microporous samples of PCN-250 were obtained, but they displayed highly decreased pore volumes and surface areas when compared to pristine PCN250 (Table S2 and Figure S4). Thus, the addition of C3 and C14 carboxylic acids hardly changed the pore structure of PCN-250. In contrast, the use of hexanoic acid (C6) and nonanoic acid (C9) both significantly changed the porosity of the obtained MOF samples by generating HP-MOFs. The PCN-250(C6) displays a clear Type IV “S-Shaped” isotherm, with a pore diameter between 4-8 nm (Figure 2d). However, the PCN-250(C9) shows a Type I isotherm, with both a 4 nm and a much larger pore diameter between 6-18 nm (Figure 2e).
In order to investigate how the concentration of fatty acids would affect the mesopore structure within the obtained MOFs, both C6 and C9 were used in three different concentrations (0.7M, 1.4M, and 2.1M). When the acid of choice is used in 0.7 M [PCN250(C6-0.7M) and PCN-250(C9-0.7M)] or 1.4M [PCN-250(C6- 1.4M) and PCN-250(C9-1.4M)] concentrations, all four of the generated samples display a clear Type IV or $^{\mathrm{{s}}}$ -Shaped” isotherm (Figures 2a and 2b), which indicates an HP-MOF structure. However, when the acid concentration was increased to 2.1M, both PCN-250(C6-2.1M) and PCN-250(C9-2.1M) displayed a Type I isotherm (Figure 2c), indicating only microporous structures were obtained.
When taking a deeper look at the ${\sf N}_{2}$ isotherms of the four different obtained HP-MOFs, differences in their desorption curves (hysteresis loops) can be seen. PCN-250(C6-0.7M), PCN250(C9-0.7M) and PCN-250(C6-1.4M) all display a desorption hysteresis in the ${\sf N}_{2}$ isotherm that is consistent with an H2-type loop. H2 hysteresis loops are known to signal the presence of pores with narrow mouths (Ink Bottle Pores) or internal mesopores with little to no direct connectivity to the particle surface.[32] However, PCN-250(C9-1.4M) displays another type of hysteresis, named H1. H1-type hysteresis loops are reported to signal cyclical pore geometries as well as mesopores that are connected to the surface of the particle which yields the guest molecules experiencing little to no pore blocking effects.[32–35]
Furthermore, using the Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) pore size model applied to the desorption cycles, the four HP-MOFs display different pore sizes as well. PCN-250(C6-0.7M), PCN250(C9-0.7M), and PCN-250(C6-1.4M) all display mesopore sizes of approximately $4{\cdot}6\ \mathsf{n m}$ in diameter, while PCN-250(C9- 1.4M) displays much larger mesopores of approximately $8-16~\mathsf{n m}$ in diameter (Figures 2d and 2e). In addition, SEM was performed on the PCN-250(C6-1.4M) and PCN-250(C9-1.4M) in order to gain information on mesopore location within the MOF crystals. As shown in Figure 3f, visual mesopores or gaps can be seen on the surface of the particles of PCN-250(C9-1.4M). In contrast, PCN-250(C6-1.4M) particles (Figure 3e) display an extremely smooth surface with no visual evidence of mesopores.
Furthermore, with the evidence from isotherm types, hysteresis types, pore sizes, and SEM micrographs, it has been shown that there are two distinct mesopore structures that can be obtained by varying the length and concentration of monocarboxylic acid molecules utilized during the solvothermal synthesis. It was hypothesized that there could be two different mechanisms of mesopore formation within HP-MOFs using mono-carboxylic acid molecules. The first, as shown in Figure 1, is a modulation-based mechanism, which is responsible for the structure of PCN-250(C6-0.7M), PCN-250(C9-0.7M), and PCN250(C6-1.4M). Mechanism 2, which is thought to be responsible for the structure of PCN-250(C9-1.4M) (Figure 1) is a templatingbased mechanism.
In order to examine if a templating mechanism predominates in the formation of PCN-250(C9-1.4M), it was proposed that micelles or other self-assembled aggregates could be characterized in the synthetic solutions of each MOF. In order to obtain this evidence, Dynamic Light Scattering (DLS) experiments were at $90^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ . (Experiments were performed at 90C due to the nature of DLS measurements. In order for the data to be calculated, the viscosity and refractive index of the base solvent is needed at the measurements temperature and $90^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ was the highest temperature found in the literature.[36]) As shown in Table S3, no particles were observed in any of the 0.7M, 1.4M and 2.1M C3 synthetic solutions. Furthermore, in the C14 synthetic solutions, no micelle type particles were observed in 0.7M solution but were observed in the 1.4 and $2.1\mathsf{M}$ solution of approximately 60 and 160 nm respectively. Furthermore, as noted by the large Z-Average reported in Table S3 for the C14 synthetic solutions, thoursands of nanomaters, are thought be measured from the formation of self-assembled bilayers formed by the aggregation of the hydrophobic chain of myristic acid molecules. Moreover, the formation of these mystic acid containing bilayers make the C14 synthetic solutions extremely viscous, which are not ideal conditions for MOF synthesis. Moreover, these poor MOF growth conditions can help explain why the crystallinity, gas uptake and surface area of PCN-250(C14-X.XM) MOFs are significantly lower than expected.
More importantly, no micelle sized particles were detected in any of the PCN-250(C6-X.XM) synthetic solutions whereas relatively small, nanometer-sized particles were detected in the PCN-250(C9-1.4M) and PCN-250(C9-2.1M). These particles are thought to be micelles generated by the self-assembly of nonanoic acid in the presence of a polar solvent such as DMF. Secondly, it is work noting, the Z-average of all the C9 solutions is in the hundreds of nanometers range. This is due to the presence of some smaller bilayers type self-assemled structures which although do form, due to their small size in comparison to the sizes measured in the C14 solutions, do not effect MOFs generation to the same degree. We believe that this data falls in line with the findings of previously reported literature that micelles can, in fact, be formed in DMF if the surfactant has the correct molecular length and concentrations, furthermore, previous reports show that the length and concentration of the surfactant (fatty acid) help govern the critical micelles concentration (cmc) and the formation of micelles in DMF, which are significantly higher than in aqueous solutions.[18,19,37] Lastly, for PCN-250(C9- 1.4M), the micelles generated were of approximately 11 nm, which is in good agreement with the calculated pore size distribution for PCN-250(C9-1.4M).
In order to show the how the different hierarchal structures could have an impact on the applications of these MOFs, the ability PCN-250(C9-1.4M) and PCN-250(C6-1.4M) to remove organic dyes from aqueous solutions was tested. Methylene Blue (MB) was employed as a model dye, and UV−Vis spectroscopy was implemented to monitor the adsorption process of MB from an aqueous solution under vigorous stirring at ambient temperature and pressure. The initial concentration of the MB solution was $10{\mathrm{~mg/L}},$ and during the adsorption process, various time points were taken in order to gain an insight into the kinetics of the adsorption process. Figure 5b and 5c both show PCN250(C6-1.4M) and PCN-250(C9-1.4M) displaying good adsorption kinetics, removing $65\%$ and $48\%$ of the MB, respectively, after only 5 minutes. Furthermore, after 2 hours of adsorption time, $99\%$ removal of MB from the aqueous solution was achieved by both samples.
In order to validate the effect the different mesoporous structures have on the adsorption performance of PCN-250 for MB, the same test was performed using a microporous PCN250(C3-1.4M). As seen from Figure S23, PCN-250(C6-1.4M) and PCN-250(C9-1.4M) significantly outperform the microporous PCN-250(C3-1.4M). PCN-250(C3-1.4M) can remove approximately $21\%$ of the MB dye within the first 5 minutes, but then significantly slows down. After 1 hour and 6 hours, there was $33\%$ and $45\%$ MB removed, respectively. The low adsorption capacity and kinetics of the microporous sample can be attributed to surface pore blocking, whereas the mesoporous samples do not have such limitation. Additionally, to show the broad range of cationic dyes that PCN-250(C9-1.4M) can extract from an aqueous solution, Crystal Violet (CV) and Rhodamine B (RB) were also tested. As seen in Figure S24 and S25, both dyes were effectively $100\%$ sequestered in under 2 hours. Furthermore, the adoption capacities of PCN-250(C6-1.4M) and PCN-250(C9- 1.4M) were determined to be $97\ \mathsf{m g}/\mathsf{g}$ and $134\ m g/{\mathfrak{g}}$ for MB respectively. Further comparison of these MOFs is reported in Table S4.
Moreover, the effect of the mesopore type on the dye desorption process and the recyclability of the adsorbents was investigated using SEM. After the adsorption of the MB dye, the MOF samples were isolated via centrifugation and washed with $25~\mathsf{m L}$ of DMF. Following the DMF wash, the samples were dried in air at $75^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 6 hours, then SEM micrographs were obtained and compared to the pre-adsorption micrographs. PCN-250(C6- 1.4M) and PCN-250(C9-1.4M) have a fairly uniform particle size and morphology (Figures 3a and 3b). Following the adsorption and desorption of the dye, PCN-250(C9-1.4M) maintains its crystal integrity while PCN-250(C6-1.4M) does not (Figures 5d and 5e). Figure 5d reveals that crystals were broken apart into various smaller sizes and shapes, which can be explained as the result of the exothermic dye desorption process paired with internal mesopores. The internal mesopores do not allow for facile dye desorption, while the presence of surface mesopores in PCN250(C9-1.4M) does permit facile dye desorption, leading to the preservation of crystal morphology.
Due to the loss of PCN-250(C6-1.4M) crystal integrity, the dye adsorption performance also suffered. As shown in Figure 5b, PCN-250(C6-1.4M) adsorption kinetics slowed with subsequent adsorption experiments, signifying that not only does the presence of mesopores affect dye adsorption process when compared to a microporous sample, but mesopore location and access to the crystal surface also affects the recyclability of a MOF adsorbent. Given that ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption data of the above two HP_MOFs exhibits similar gas uptake capacity, the small gas molecule hardly tell the difference between the two mesopores. However, the recyclability in dye adsorption experiments provids indeepth view for distinguishing two similar mesopores in MOF absorbents. Considering that the size of the gas and dye molecuels are different, their affinity to the MOF frameworks are also distinct, allowing for verifying subtely difference in the aspect of pore environment and stability. Here, the combination of modulating and templating method to form HP-MOFs allows us to finely tune the porosity. At the same thime, the combination of gas and dye molecule adsorption builds the touchstone for discriminating the subtle difference of the pores in the porous materials.
In conclusion, for the first time, we have introduced tunable hierarchical pores into PCN-250 by using the templating method. The obtained HP-MOFs exhibit completely different mesoporosity in size, volume, and position. By varying the length and concentration of the templates, hierarchally porous PCN-250 can be modified to have 4-6 nm and 12-16 nm diameter mesopores. More importantly, surface and internal pores can be introduced to the PCN-250. Because of the tunable porosity, the gas adsorption property of the obtained PCN-250 was subtly tuned. Moreover, the PCN-250 with surface and internal pores show significantly different dye removal performance. PCN-250, with both surface and internal mesopores, displays great recyclability by removing $99\%$ of MB over three cycles. However, PCN-250, with only the internal mesopores displays, poor recyclability, removing as low as $37\%$ of MB. This research gains insights for creating mesopores in traditional microporous MOF adsorbents via the templating method. Further investigation will provide a universal toolkit for introducing tunable porous materials in a facile synthetic approach.
## Experimental Section
General PCN-250 Synthesis: Solution 1, which consisted of the . $720{\mathfrak{g}}$ of $\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{N O}2)3(\mathsf{H}2\mathsf{O})6$ , $8~\mathrm{mL}$ of Dimethylformamide (DMF), and X mL of the desired fatty acid was heated to $100^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 1 Hour. Next, Solution 2 was prepared which consisted of . $\scriptstyle240\ g\ N a4{\mathsf{A B T C}}$ dissolved in $8~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF and heat to $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 1 hour. Next, the solutions were added together in an enclosed reaction vessel $40~\mathrm{mL}$ vial) and heated to $150^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 12 hours. After 12 hours, the reaction was allowed to cool to room temperature and the sample was collected vial centrifugation. The products were then washed $_{3\times}$ with DMF with the final wash being followed by immersing the product in DMF for 12 hours. Following the 12 hours, the As Synthesized (AsSyn) MOF product was obtained. In order to remove the acid modulators/templates form the structure, $250\mathrm{mg}$ of the sample was added to $\mathsf{a}20\mathsf{m L}$ vial. Next 8 mL of a solution of $25\%$ glacial acetic acid and $75\%$ DMF was prepared was added to the vial. This solution was allowed to sit at $25\mathsf{C}$ , for the desired time (e.g. 1hr. 12hr, 24hr, etc…). After the desired time the product was collected via centrifugation then washed $_{3\times}$ with DMF with the final wash being followed by immersing the product in DMF for 12 hours. This was repeated with methanol (MeOH) wash 3x with the final wash being a solvent exchange at RT for 48 hours. Lastly, the product was dried at $75^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 6 hours.
Dye Adsorption experiments: Cycle 1 was performed using $80~\mathsf{m g}$ of the selected adsorbent (No further drying or activating was performed prior to starting experiments). The adsorbent was put into an $80~\mathrm{{\mL}}$ Celstir apparatus (Purchased from DWK Life Sciences) along with $80~\mathrm{mL}$ of 10 $\mathsf{m g}/\mathsf{L}$ Methylene Blue solution. The apparatus was stirred using a magnetic stir plate at approximately 200 rpm and various time points were taken during this time. For a specific time point, 1 mL of solution was removed using a 1 mL syringe and filtered through $\mathsf{a}2\upmu\mathsf{m}$ syringe filter. This solution was diluted with 1 mL of $H_{2}O$ , and then a UV-Vis spectrum was obtained to determine the concentration of MB in solution. Following the selected time, the adsorbent was collected via centrifugation and soaked in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF for 2 hours (twice repeated) in order to desorb the MB dye. Next, the adsorbent was solvent exchanged with Methanol $(20~\mathsf{m L})$ for 12 hours and then subsequently dried in an oven at $75^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 6 hours. Cycles 2 and 3 were then performed in the same manner as Cycle 1, using $60~\mathsf{m g}$ of adsorbent and $60~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ MB solution for Cycle 2 and $40\mathrm{\mg}$ adsorbent and $40~\mathrm{mg/L}$ MB for Cycle 3.
